/-!
# Printed sentences that are records, not claims

Most sentences of the two manuscripts are graded by
`scripts/sentence_census.py` against a Lean declaration.  Some cannot be, and
should not be: a sentence that credits somebody else's theorem, states that a
question is open, or discloses how the work was produced makes no mathematical
assertion of its own, and writing a `Prop` for it would be a claim the paper
does not make.

Those sentences still deserve a counterpart in the development, so that "every
sentence of the manuscript has something in Lean answering to it" is literally
true rather than true-except-for-a-residue.  This module gives them one **as
data**.  `SentenceRecord` has no `Prop` field and no proof: it is a record of
what the sentence says and why no theorem stands behind it.  Nothing here can
be mistaken for a formalization, because there is nothing here to prove.

## What coverage means

For each manuscript, coverage is the union of three disjoint things:

* the census rows graded `formalized`, `definition`, `ledger` or `partial`,
  which name declarations;
* the census rows graded `structural`, which fix binders, signpost, or lead
  into a display, and whose counterpart IS the census row -- recording them
  here as well would triple this file and add nothing anyone can check;
* the records below, which are the `attribution`, `open` and `provenance` rows.

`scripts/sentence_records.py --check` enforces that the union is exact in both
directions: every record here corresponds to a census row with the matching
status, and every census row with one of those three statuses has a record.  A
sentence cannot fall between the three by being dropped from one and never
added to the other.

## Generated

`NonMFSentenceRecords.lean` and `MFRecognitionSentenceRecords.lean` are
generated by `scripts/sentence_records.py` from the sentence maps.  Edit the
maps, not those files.  This module, which holds only the types, is written by
hand.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace Manuscript
namespace Meta

/-- Why a printed sentence has no theorem behind it.

`openProblem` rather than `open`, which is a keyword. -/
inductive SentenceStatus
  /-- The sentence credits or dates other people's work. -/
  | attribution
  /-- The sentence says that something is unknown; no declaration could
  settle it. -/
  | openProblem
  /-- The sentence discloses how the work was produced; it quotes rather than
  asserts. -/
  | provenance
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- One printed sentence that is a record rather than a claim.

`key` is the sentence key `scripts/sentence_census.py` computes, so a record
and its census row are the same object seen from two sides.  `tex` is the
sentence as printed, normalized the way the census normalizes it.  There is
deliberately no field of type `Prop`. -/
structure SentenceRecord where
  /-- The census key: the first twelve hex digits of the sha256 of the
  normalized sentence. -/
  key : String
  /-- Why no declaration stands behind the sentence. -/
  status : SentenceStatus
  /-- The sentence, as printed. -/
  tex : String
  /-- The census note recording the judgement. -/
  note : String
  deriving Repr

end Meta
end Manuscript
end GroupApproximation
